  "notes": {
    "methodology": "Rolling window OLS/Ridge regression. Each OOS week: re-estimate on prior 400 weeks, forecast next week RV.",
    "vrp_definition": "VRP = VIX - annualized 4-week realized vol. Positive VRP = implied > realized (normal).",
    "ridge_rationale": "Ridge(alpha=1) for forecasting stability; OLS for significance testing (t-stats).",
    "qlike_interpretation": "QLIKE is scale-invariant and preferred for variance forecasting (Patton 2011). Positive DM t-stat = VRP model WORSE than comparator.",
    "limitations": [
      "Only SPY tested (single asset)",
      "VRP proxy uses 4-week rolling RV (not intraday estimator)",
      "Ridge alpha=1 not optimized per period",
      "Weekly frequency limits sample size per OOS period",
      "Linear VRP model can produce negative variance forecasts (clipped to 1e-10), causing QLIKE explosion in 3/5 periods"
    ]
  },
  "critical_interpretation": {
    "headline": "VRP is statistically significant but a POOR OOS forecaster — classic significance vs forecasting disconnect",
    "vrp_in_sample_significance": "4/5 periods have 100% |t|>2 (Period 1 = 25.7%). VRP coefficient is ALWAYS positive (99-100%). Mean t-stat=3.33. This means VRP genuinely contains information about next-week RV.",
    "vrp_oos_forecasting_failure": "VRP model wins 0/5 QLIKE, 0/5 MSE. In 3/5 periods QLIKE explodes (values 222K-2.5M) due to near-zero/negative forecasts clipped to 1e-10. DM tests show VRP WORSE than both baseline and VIX-only (all positive t-stats).",
    "vix_only_dominance": "VIX-only wins MSE in 4/5 periods and QLIKE in 2/5. MZ R² for VIX-only: 0.38-0.51 (excellent calibration). VIX-only has mean t-stat 16-23 (overwhelmingly significant).",
    "root_cause": "VRP's marginal IS R² improvement is tiny (0.9-2.9%) over baseline. The linear OLS/Ridge model is fragile — when VRP is noisy (as in COVID/post-COVID), forecasts can go negative, destroying QLIKE. VIX alone already captures most of the variance information.",
    "key_insight": "This confirms K457's QLIKE ceiling: VIX is the sufficient statistic for weekly variance forecasting. VRP has statistical significance but NO forecasting value — it is redundant given VIX. This is consistent with 21 prior confirmations of VIX sufficiency.",
    "publication_verdict": "VRP weekly is NOT publication-worthy as a forecasting improvement. However, the SIGNIFICANCE (100% |t|>2 in 4/5 periods) IS notable — it documents the existence of a time-varying risk premium. For the paper: report as 'VRP exists and is persistent, but does not improve forecasting beyond VIX alone.'",
    "comparison_with_k457": "K457 reported VRP 100% |t|>2 significant but QLIKE=962 (worst). K459 confirms this across all 5 periods: significance YES, forecasting NO. The discrepancy is resolved."
  }
}